TEXT_FILES = {'README.md': '# What Players Value: Destiny 2 vs Warframe\n\nCTS*2000 — Scripting for the Humanities, University of Guelph, Fall 2026.\n\nWe compare recommended and not-recommended Steam reviews of Destiny 2 and\nWarframe. Our working notebook currently focuses on monetization language,\nkeyword frequencies, percentages, and close reading of review examples.\n\n## Start here\n\n| I want to… | Open |\n| --- | --- |\n| Know what to do next | [Task board](docs/task_board.md) |\n| Understand the assignment | [Requirements and deadlines](docs/assignment_requirements.md) |\n| Read our question and scope | [Project brief](docs/project_brief.md) |\n| Run the analysis | [Notebook instructions](notebooks/README.md) |\n| Check the current results | [Findings and limitations](docs/current_findings.md) |\n| Understand the dataset | [Data guide](data/README.md) |\n| Record my work | [Contributions](docs/contributions.md) |\n| Prepare the paper | [Paper outline](paper/outline.md) |\n| Prepare the presentation | [Presentation plan](presentation/run_of_show.md) |\n| Update the group | [Check-ins](docs/check_ins.md) |\n| Document AI assistance | [AI record](docs/ai_usage.md) |\n\n## Current status\n\n- The annotated working notebook is included unchanged.\n- The Destiny and Warframe manual-check CSVs are included.\n- Results listed here come from saved notebook outputs, not a fresh run on\n  the original Excel files. A complete rerun is still pending.\n- A spending-keyword match does not automatically mean a pricing complaint.\n- The proposal also covers gameplay and story. Broader theme comparisons\n  have not been completed in the current notebook; agree on final scope.\n- A final data visualization, presentation notebook, research paper, and\n  complete contribution/process records still need to be prepared.\n\n## Team\n\nKenzo Miller · Rhea Castelino · Emily Ngo · Aaliyah Bajaj · Jordan Van Remortel\n\nThese names come from the topic statement. GitHub handles and completed\ncontributions must be recorded by the group; they are not inferred here.\n\n## Run the existing notebook\n\nOpen `notebooks/Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb` in Google Colab. Run the\nupload cell, select the two original Excel files, then run cells in order.\nThe notebook uses `google.colab.files`; this setup does not convert it to a\nCodespaces or local Jupyter workflow.\n\n## Upcoming submission requirements\n\n| Deliverable | Requirement | Deadline in course outline |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Presentation | One live Jupyter Notebook; 10 minutes + 10-minute Q&A | Final presentation week, Nov. 30 / Dec. 2 / Dec. 4; group slot TBD |\n| Research paper | 5–7 pages; at least one visualization; bibliography and author contributions | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. Toronto time |\n| GitHub repository | Original code, annotation, experiments and identifiable contributions | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. Toronto time |\n\nDates are from the supplied course outline. Use a later instructor update\nif the schedule has changed. The setup does not mark any submission done.\n\n## Working together\n\nRead [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Make small, identifiable commits,\nexplain changes in pull requests, and record decisions and intermediate\nresults. The task board is a Markdown file in this repository; it is not a\nlive GitHub Projects board.\n', 'docs/project_home.md': '# What Players Value: Destiny 2 vs Warframe\n\nCTS*2000 — Scripting for the Humanities, University of Guelph, Fall 2026.\n\nWe compare recommended and not-recommended Steam reviews of Destiny 2 and\nWarframe. Our working notebook currently focuses on monetization language,\nkeyword frequencies, percentages, and close reading of review examples.\n\n## Start here\n\n| I want to… | Open |\n| --- | --- |\n| Know what to do next | [Task board](task_board.md) |\n| Understand the assignment | [Requirements and deadlines](assignment_requirements.md) |\n| Read our question and scope | [Project brief](project_brief.md) |\n| Run the analysis | [Notebook instructions](../notebooks/README.md) |\n| Check the current results | [Findings and limitations](current_findings.md) |\n| Understand the dataset | [Data guide](../data/README.md) |\n| Record my work | [Contributions](contributions.md) |\n| Prepare the paper | [Paper outline](../paper/outline.md) |\n| Prepare the presentation | [Presentation plan](../presentation/run_of_show.md) |\n| Update the group | [Check-ins](check_ins.md) |\n| Document AI assistance | [AI record](ai_usage.md) |\n\n## Current status\n\n- The annotated working notebook is included unchanged.\n- The Destiny and Warframe manual-check CSVs are included.\n- Results listed here come from saved notebook outputs, not a fresh run on\n  the original Excel files. A complete rerun is still pending.\n- A spending-keyword match does not automatically mean a pricing complaint.\n- The proposal also covers gameplay and story. Broader theme comparisons\n  have not been completed in the current notebook; agree on final scope.\n- A final data visualization, presentation notebook, research paper, and\n  complete contribution/process records still need to be prepared.\n\n## Team\n\nKenzo Miller · Rhea Castelino · Emily Ngo · Aaliyah Bajaj · Jordan Van Remortel\n\nThese names come from the topic statement. GitHub handles and completed\ncontributions must be recorded by the group; they are not inferred here.\n\n## Run the existing notebook\n\nOpen `notebooks/Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb` in Google Colab. Run the\nupload cell, select the two original Excel files, then run cells in order.\nThe notebook uses `google.colab.files`; this setup does not convert it to a\nCodespaces or local Jupyter workflow.\n\n## Upcoming submission requirements\n\n| Deliverable | Requirement | Deadline in course outline |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Presentation | One live Jupyter Notebook; 10 minutes + 10-minute Q&A | Final presentation week, Nov. 30 / Dec. 2 / Dec. 4; group slot TBD |\n| Research paper | 5–7 pages; at least one visualization; bibliography and author contributions | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. Toronto time |\n| GitHub repository | Original code, annotation, experiments and identifiable contributions | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. Toronto time |\n\nDates are from the supplied course outline. Use a later instructor update\nif the schedule has changed. The setup does not mark any submission done.\n\n## Working together\n\nRead [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md). Make small, identifiable commits,\nexplain changes in pull requests, and record decisions and intermediate\nresults. The task board is a Markdown file in this repository; it is not a\nlive GitHub Projects board.\n', 'docs/assignment_requirements.md': "# Assignment requirements\n\nSource: `CTS2000 Course Outline(1).pdf`, supplied by the group, pages 4–8.\nThis is a working transcription of the requirements, not a replacement\nfor the original brief or later CourseLink announcements.\n\n| Deliverable | Weight | What the outline requires | Due |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Topic statement | 10% | 2 pages; motivation, question(s), dataset and relevance; members and equitable division of labour outside page count | Oct. 9, 2026, 5 p.m. |\n| Research paper | 20% | 5–7 pages; question, context, data, methods, supported answer, visualization, limitations and future research | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. |\n| Repository | 10% | GitHub used regularly for original code, careful annotations, experiments, intermediate results and identifiable individual contributions | Dec. 7, 2026, 5 p.m. |\n| Presentation | 10% | One Jupyter Notebook, run live; 10-minute talk + 10-minute Q&A | Final week; exact group slot TBD |\n\nTimes use America/Toronto. The outline lists presentation sessions on\nNov. 30, Dec. 2 and Dec. 4. Group check-ins are listed for Weeks 9–11;\nWeek 11 is Nov. 23 / Nov. 25. No exact check-in cutoff is given here.\n\n## Research paper checklist\n\n- [ ] State one concrete question and situate it in secondary scholarship.\n- [ ] Describe the dataset, selection and preparation.\n- [ ] Explain the methods and why they answer the question.\n- [ ] Support the answer with evidence from the analysis.\n- [ ] Include at least one data visualization and explain its significance.\n- [ ] Discuss limitations, unsuccessful methods and missing data.\n- [ ] Suggest future research directions.\n- [ ] Edit the paper into a coherent shared voice.\n- [ ] Add a bibliography and an author contribution statement.\n\nBibliography and author contributions are outside the stated page count.\nEach student writes one or two sentences about their real contributions.\n\n## Presentation checklist\n\nThe final presentation notebook must contain three sections:\n\n1. Dataset and research question.\n2. Methods.\n3. Answer supported by analysis and visualization.\n\n- [ ] Test the notebook live with the source data.\n- [ ] Rehearse a 10-minute talk.\n- [ ] Prepare for 10 minutes of questions.\n- [ ] Confirm the group assigned to us for kickstarting its Q&A.\n\n## Collaboration and process records\n\nEach member should contribute substantively. The outline explicitly says\ndivision of labour should not simply mean one person codes while another\nperson writes. Record actual work in commits, pull requests, check-ins\nand contribution statements. Creating this scaffold does not establish\nanyone's completed contribution or reconstruct earlier GitHub history.\n\nFormal assignments normally use CourseLink DropBox; a repository or pull\nrequest does not by itself establish that a submission was made.\n\n## AI records required by the outline\n\nRecord tool/model version, date/time, prompts and output; retain recoverable\nversions and distinguish AI assistance from the group's own contribution.\nUse [ai_usage.md](ai_usage.md) and add the missing earlier records. This is\ndocumentation work, not a requirement to ask permission for routine edits.\n", 'docs/project_brief.md': "# Project brief\n\n## Project and source proposal\n\nWorking repository title: **What Players Value: Destiny 2 vs Warframe**.\n\nThe topic statement is titled *What Players Value: Comparing Recommended\nand Non-Recommended MMORPG Steam Reviews*. Its motivating questions include\ndifferences in monetization, gameplay and story discussions between review\nrecommendation groups.\n\n## Scope decision for the group\n\nOriginal broader question:\n\n> How do discussions of monetization, gameplay, and story differ between\n> recommended and non-recommended reviews?\n\nSuggested narrower question that matches the current implemented analysis:\n\n> How does spending-related language differ between recommended and\n> not-recommended Steam reviews of Destiny 2 and Warframe, and what do\n> contextual checks reveal about those keyword matches?\n\nThe narrower wording is a proposal, not a recorded group decision. Agree\neither to narrow the final question or to finish the gameplay/story analysis.\nDo not describe unimplemented themes as completed findings.\n\n## Dataset and methods\n\n- Two game files, 1,000 rows each, from Thomas Byers's Steam review dataset.\n- Use review text and Steam's `voted_up` recommendation label.\n- Drop missing review text, lowercase words, trim punctuation and remove\n  the notebook's short stopword list.\n- Count selected words and compare normalized frequencies.\n- Compare the percentage of reviews mentioning spending keywords.\n- Read examples in context and record manual complaint judgements.\n\nRecommendation status is Steam's recommend/not-recommend label; the code\ndoes not automatically classify every sentence's emotional sentiment.\nThis is descriptive analysis of a historical dataset, not proof of game\nquality, review validity, player motivation, or internet culture's causal\ninfluence. Those broader ideas need evidence beyond the current counts.\n\n## Evidence locations\n\n- Code: [working notebook](../notebooks/Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb)\n- Saved results and limitations: [current findings](current_findings.md)\n- Task ownership: [task board](task_board.md)\n- Original topic statement: supplied group document; upload the original\n  separately if the team wants it in this repository.\n", 'data/README.md': "# Data guide\n\n## Source\n\nThomas Byers, *Steam User Review Data – 1,000 Most Recent Reviews from\n500 Popular Games*.\n\n- [Dataset page](https://figshare.unimelb.edu.au/articles/dataset/Steam_User_Review_Data_1_000_Most_Recent_Reviews_from_500_Popular_Games/30842609)\n- [DOI](https://doi.org/10.26188/30842609.v1)\n- [Publisher metadata](https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles/30842609)\n- Published: December 15, 2025.\n- Collection described by the publisher: December 6, 2023.\n- Listed licence: CC0.\n\nPublisher metadata was checked while preparing this setup. It describes\nrecent reviews and games ordered by organic visibility/relevance at\ncollection, rather than a random sample of all Steam users. Avoid treating\nour subset as representative of all current players.\n\n## Exact input filenames expected by the existing notebook\n\n| Game | File | Sheet |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Destiny 2 | `7_Destiny_2_1085660xlsx.xlsx` | `Reviews` |\n| Warframe | `16_Warframe_230410.xlsx` | `Reviews` |\n\nThe spelling of the Destiny filename matches the current code. Do not\nsilently rename it or alter the loader during repository organization.\n\nThe source spreadsheets are not bundled in this setup. Add your existing\ncopies to `data/raw/` locally and upload them when Colab's upload cell asks.\nThe default ignore rules keep those spreadsheets out of commits; the group\nmay change that choice deliberately after agreeing on data sharing.\n\n## Fields used by the current analysis\n\n| Field | Meaning/use |\n| --- | --- |\n| `review` | Review text; missing values are excluded from text analysis |\n| `voted_up` | True = recommended; False = not recommended |\n\nThe dataset contains other metadata, but do not claim those fields were\nanalysed just because they exist. Language filtering and duplicate handling\nare not explicitly implemented in the current notebook; document decisions\nbefore adding either.\n\n## Cleaning limitations\n\n`dropna()` removes missing text, not necessarily blank strings. Cleaning\nsplits on whitespace, trims punctuation at token edges and lowercases.\nIt does not perform stemming, lemmatization or a complete language filter.\nThe match for a word is an exact cleaned-token match, so spelling variants\nand hyphenation can affect results. Never manually edit raw reviews.\n", 'data/raw/README.md': '# Original spreadsheets\n\nPut local copies of the two original Excel files here. They are not included\nin this scaffold. See [the data guide](../README.md) for exact filenames.\nThe unmodified notebook still asks you to upload both files in Google Colab.\n', 'notebooks/README.md': '# Analysis notebooks\n\n## Canonical working notebook\n\n[Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb](Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb)\n\nThe setup includes the latest annotated notebook available in the project\nconversation. Its 31 code cells, calculations and saved outputs are retained.\n\n## Run it in Google Colab\n\n1. Open the notebook in Google Colab using its GitHub URL or upload it.\n2. Run the first cell and upload both original Excel files.\n3. Confirm the filenames match the [data guide](../data/README.md).\n4. Run the remaining cells from top to bottom.\n5. Keep the downloaded Destiny and Warframe CSVs in `results/tables/`.\n6. Save the notebook back to the working branch and describe the change.\n\n`google.colab.files` is specific to Colab. The current notebook is not\npromised to run unchanged in local Jupyter or Codespaces. Repository setup\nruns there using standard Python; analysis still uses the Colab workflow.\n\n## Important steps\n\n- Example review cleaning and word counts.\n- Destiny recommendation split and normalized keyword frequencies.\n- Warframe recommendation split and review percentages.\n- A broader spending-word list and contextual review inspection.\n- Five sampled Destiny matches and all two Warframe matches, manually coded.\n- Separate CSV exports and a final manual-check comparison table.\n\n## Presentation notebook\n\nNot prepared yet. Build the final live notebook from the working analysis\nonce the question, chart and findings are agreed. Follow the three sections\nin [the presentation plan](../presentation/run_of_show.md). Avoid competing\nfiles named final_v2/final_final; use Git history and clear notebook roles.\n', 'docs/current_findings.md': '# Current findings and limitations\n\n**Evidence status:** these are the saved outputs in the supplied working\nnotebook. The original spreadsheets were not available for a complete fresh\nrun during setup. Reproduce and verify before using the figures as final.\n\n## Review groups with non-missing text\n\n| Game | Recommended | Not recommended | Total with text |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Destiny 2 | 521 | 476 | 997 |\n| Warframe | 938 | 60 | 998 |\n\nEach spreadsheet originally contains 1,000 rows. The excluded-text counts\nare 3 for Destiny and 2 for Warframe in the saved outputs.\n\n## Percentage of reviews mentioning the exact token `money`\n\n| Game | Recommendation | Matches | Group total | Percentage |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Destiny 2 | Recommended | 16 | 521 | 3.07% |\n| Destiny 2 | Not recommended | 85 | 476 | 17.86% |\n| Warframe | Recommended | 21 | 938 | 2.24% |\n| Warframe | Not recommended | 1 | 60 | 1.67% |\n\n## Broader spending-keyword search\n\n| Game | Recommendation | Matching reviews | Group total | Percentage |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Destiny 2 | Recommended | 54 | 521 | 10.36% |\n| Destiny 2 | Not recommended | 173 | 476 | 36.34% |\n| Warframe | Recommended | 62 | 938 | 6.61% |\n| Warframe | Not recommended | 2 | 60 | 3.33% |\n\nEach review is counted once if it matches at least one spending keyword.\nThese are percentages of reviews, unlike the earlier per-1,000-word table.\n\n## Manual review checks\n\n| Game | Negative keyword matches | Checked | Coded as clear monetization complaints |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Destiny 2 | 173 | 5, sampled with seed 42 | 5 |\n| Warframe | 2 | Both matching reviews | 0 |\n\nThe Warframe reviews concern quest design/time spent waiting and a ban/\nsupport issue. The labels are manual judgements encoded in the notebook;\nno classifier produced them. The lists of labels depend on those same\nexamples in the same order and must be checked if inputs change.\n\n## Interpretation supported by these results\n\nSpending keywords occur more often in the negative Destiny group in this\ndataset. Reading the matches shows why the terms need contextual checking:\na word such as `spend` can refer to time, and a mention of money can appear\nin a complaint about a ban. Keyword matches are not complaint counts.\n\n## Limits to keep in the paper and presentation\n\n- Five checked Destiny reviews are not an estimate of complaints across all\n  476 negative Destiny reviews or all Steam players.\n- No pricing complaints in two checked Warframe matches does not establish\n  that Warframe has no monetization complaints.\n- The games have very different recommendation-group sizes.\n- Exact keywords can miss synonyms and capture unrelated uses.\n- First matching examples are illustrative, not random samples.\n- Review labels can coexist with mixed or ironic opinions.\n- Historical collection and relevance ordering limit representativeness.\n- No causal relationship or current game-quality ranking is established.\n- The current notebook does not complete the broader gameplay/story scope.\n\n## Suggested chart task — not yet implemented\n\nMake a grouped bar chart showing the percentage of reviews matching the\nbroader spending list, by game and recommendation status. Display group\nsizes and label it as keyword mentions, not confirmed complaints. Reproduce\nthe source counts first and use methods the group can explain.\n', 'docs/task_board.md': "# Task board\n\nUpdate this Markdown board after work is completed. Proposed leads below\nfollow interests in the topic statement; they are not confirmed assignments\nor records of completed work. Review ownership together.\n\n## Next actions\n\n| ID | Task | Proposed lead | Status | Evidence needed to finish |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| T01 | Agree on final research question: monetization only or finish gameplay/story | Whole group | To decide | Decision recorded in project brief and check-in |\n| T02 | Rerun the notebook on both original spreadsheets | Kenzo + a second reviewer TBD | Pending | Reproducible run, saved outputs and reviewed CSVs |\n| T03 | Review spending words and manually checked examples | Rhea + Jordan | Pending | Context notes; labels and their order checked |\n| T04 | Build and explain at least one data visualization | Emily + a coding partner TBD | Pending | Figure tied to verified notebook counts, caption and code |\n| T05 | Find and read relevant secondary scholarship | Whole group; lead TBD | Pending | Sources and notes in paper/references.md |\n| T06 | Prepare the 5–7-page shared paper | Whole group | Pending | Complete draft aligned with final question and evidence |\n| T07 | Assemble the final live presentation notebook | Kenzo + group | Pending | Three required sections and verified visualization |\n| T08 | Rehearse the talk and prepare Q&A | Whole group | Pending | 10-minute run, assigned speaking parts and confirmed slot |\n| T09 | Complete contribution and AI-process records | Every member | Pending | Accurate statements, prompts/outputs, commit/PR links |\n| T10 | Confirm Aaliyah's tasks and cross-review roles | Aaliyah + group | To agree | Roles chosen and recorded; do not infer from blank proposal |\n| T11 | Submit and check access to final paper/repository | Submission lead TBD | Pending | CourseLink submission receipt and working instructor access |\n\n## Existing work to verify\n\n| Item | Status |\n| --- | --- |\n| Working analysis, short code comments and two manual-check tables | Included; full rerun pending |\n| Destiny and Warframe manual-check CSVs | Included from saved review evidence |\n| Repository documentation scaffold | AI-assisted draft; group review pending |\n| Original topic statement | Located and read for this setup; submission status not inferred |\n\n## Ownership principle\n\nEveryone should contribute to analysis/code and interpretation/writing in\nan equitable way. Use real GitHub handles only after members provide them.\nRecord completed work in [contributions.md](contributions.md), not by\nchanging proposed leads into claims about authorship.\n\n## Board conventions\n\nStatus values: To decide → Pending → In progress → Review → Done.\nAdd a commit, pull request or output link when a task moves to Done.\nNew tasks can use the repository's issue template; existing rows remain\nthe lightweight board unless the group deliberately sets up GitHub Projects.\n", 'docs/contributions.md': '# Team contributions\n\nIntentions below come from `Group Project - Topic Statement (1).pdf`.\nThey are not claims of completed work. Every member should add accurate\nentries and review the final author statement.\n\n| Member | Stated interests / proposed contribution | GitHub handle | Confirmed completed work |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Kenzo Miller | Data preparation, Python comparisons, repository organization, methods/limits writing, presentation notebook | TBD | To record and verify |\n| Rhea Castelino | Review language, keywords/patterns, conclusions and discussion | TBD | To record and verify |\n| Emily Ngo | Theme interpretation, visualizations, Python data organization | TBD | To record and verify |\n| Aaliyah Bajaj | Role not filled in the supplied topic statement | TBD | To record and verify |\n| Jordan Van Remortel | Gameplay/story/monetization patterns, interpretation, Python review organization and writing | TBD | To record and verify |\n\n## Work log\n\n| Date | Member | Work actually completed | File / commit / PR | Reviewer |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| TBD | TBD | Add a concrete completed task | TBD | TBD |\n\n## Final paper contribution statements\n\nEach member writes one or two sentences about their actual contribution.\nDo not copy the planned roles as if all of them happened.\n\n- Kenzo Miller: TBD.\n- Rhea Castelino: TBD.\n- Emily Ngo: TBD.\n- Aaliyah Bajaj: TBD.\n- Jordan Van Remortel: TBD.\n\nEarlier work should be recorded honestly with its real date/evidence where\navailable. Do not backdate commits, assign fake coauthors, or invent work.\n', 'docs/check_ins.md': "# Group check-ins and decisions\n\nThe course outline asks for regular repository use and intermediate-result\nupdates, including group check-ins in Weeks 9–11. This template does not\nsend any Slack messages or invent earlier updates.\n\n## Current status draft — November 19, 2026\n\nThe notebook compares Destiny 2 and Warframe recommendation groups using\nspending keywords and contextual review checks. Separate Warframe output\nand CSV export have been added. The saved-output evidence is summarized in\n[current_findings.md](current_findings.md). Next steps are to agree on scope,\nrerun the source data, review manual labels and create a visualization.\n\nThis paragraph is a draft for the group to review, not a record of a check-in\nthat was sent to the instructor or Slack.\n\n## Copy for each real check-in\n\n### Date/time: TBD\n\n- Members present / contributors:\n- What changed since last check-in:\n- Intermediate result and evidence link:\n- What failed or remains uncertain:\n- Decision made and reason:\n- Next tasks, owners and intended dates:\n- Commit / pull request links:\n\n## Decisions awaiting agreement\n\n| Decision | Status | Agreed wording / evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Final question and breadth of themes | Open | TBD |\n| Keyword list and manual coding criteria | Open | TBD |\n| Data visualization and caption | Open | TBD |\n| Aaliyah's tasks and cross-review assignments | Open | TBD |\n| Presentation slot, speakers and Q&A partner | Open | TBD |\n", 'paper/outline.md': "# Research paper working outline\n\n**Status: outline, not a completed research paper.**\nRequired length: 5–7 pages. Bibliography and author contributions are outside\nthe page count. Due December 7, 2026, 5 p.m. Toronto time, per course outline.\n\n## 1. Question and context\n\nState the agreed question. Explain why comparing review recommendation\ngroups is useful, and situate the project in verified secondary scholarship.\nResolve the broader proposal versus the current monetization focus first.\n\n## 2. Dataset and preparation\n\nIdentify the dataset author, historical collection, selected game files,\ngroup sizes and exclusions. Explain what recommendation status represents\nand what was actually done about missing text, languages and duplicates.\n\n## 3. Methods\n\nExplain cleaning, stopwords, exact token matching, word frequencies,\nper-1,000-word rates, percentages of reviews and contextual/manual checks.\nMake the denominator of every measure clear. Explain the five-review\nDestiny sample and why both Warframe matches were checked.\n\n## 4. Findings and visualization\n\nUse rerun-verified evidence from the notebook. Include at least one chart\nand explain its significance. Use clear labels distinguishing mentions from\ncomplaints. Quote or paraphrase review examples with appropriate source\ncontext; do not infer all players' attitudes from these examples.\n\nFigure 1: TBD — verified spending-keyword percentages by game and label.\n\n## 5. Discussion, limitations and unsuccessful approaches\n\nDiscuss false matches, missed terms, mixed review attitudes, small manual\nchecks, unequal group sizes, missing text, historical data and selection.\nExplain why looking only for `money` was too narrow and why broadening the\nkeywords still required reading context. Describe real unsuccessful work\nonly; do not invent experiments to fill a requirement.\n\n## 6. Answer and future work\n\nAnswer the agreed question at the level the evidence supports. Identify\nfuture work such as a broader coding sample or additional theme analysis,\nclearly separated from completed analysis.\n\n## Bibliography and author contributions\n\nAdd verified sources from [references.md](references.md) and real statements\nfrom [the contribution log](../docs/contributions.md). Edit the whole paper\ninto a shared voice before submission.\n", 'paper/references.md': '# References and reading notes\n\n## Verified dataset reference\n\nByers, T. (2025). *Steam User Review Data – 1,000 Most Recent Reviews from\n500 Popular Games* [Data set]. The University of Melbourne.\nhttps://doi.org/10.26188/30842609.v1\n\nPublication date and collection date are different: published in 2025;\npublisher describes collection on December 6, 2023.\n\n## Course code adapted or consulted\n\n- [Course repository](https://github.com/mervetekgurler/cts2000_fall2026)\n- [Loops and lists](https://github.com/mervetekgurler/cts2000_fall2026/blob/main/units/week_2/2-2_iteration.ipynb)\n- [Pandas](https://github.com/mervetekgurler/cts2000_fall2026/blob/main/units/week_6/6-1_pandas.ipynb)\n- [Text cleaning and CSV export](https://github.com/mervetekgurler/cts2000_fall2026/blob/main/units/week_6/6-2_corpus_level_text_analysis_montagu.ipynb)\n\nThese are methodological resources; they do not replace the secondary\nscholarship needed to contextualize the research question.\n\n## Secondary scholarship — to research and verify\n\n| Source | Main argument / relevance | Method or finding to use | Reader | Checked citation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| TBD | TBD | TBD | TBD | No |\n\nAdd only sources someone has actually inspected. Do not generate fictional\ncitations or say a paper supports a claim without checking it.\n', 'presentation/run_of_show.md': '# Live notebook presentation plan\n\n**Status: planning template.** Confirm the actual presentation slot and\nspeaking assignments. Required duration: 10 minutes plus 10 minutes Q&A.\n\n| Time | Required notebook section | What to cover | Speaker |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0:00–2:00 | Dataset and question | Agreed scope; dataset, games, historical sample and recommendation groups | TBD |\n| 2:00–7:00 | Methods | Run selected cells live; cleaning, matching, denominators and manual context checks | TBD |\n| 7:00–10:00 | Evidence-based answer | Verified comparison, at least one visualization, supported conclusion and limitations | TBD |\n\nThese time allocations are suggestions, not additional assignment rules.\n\n## Build the final presentation notebook\n\n- [ ] Use one notebook from the code base, with the three sections above.\n- [ ] Add short Markdown explanations so the audience can follow the code.\n- [ ] Include a visualization tied to verified counts.\n- [ ] Keep the working notebook available as the source of the analysis.\n- [ ] Test data upload, cell order, tables and chart on the presenting laptop.\n- [ ] Be ready to explain which labels were manual and how examples were chosen.\n- [ ] Rehearse within ten minutes while actually running the notebook.\n\n## Q&A preparation\n\n- Why these two games and these reviews?\n- Why compare percentages instead of raw counts alone?\n- How does a per-1,000-word measure differ from a percentage of reviews?\n- Why does mentioning `spend` or `money` not always mean a pricing complaint?\n- Why five Destiny examples and only two Warframe examples?\n- What can this historical subset establish, and what can it not establish?\n- How does the final research question match the analyses actually completed?\n\nOur assigned peer group and the questions we will ask it: TBD.\n', 'results/README.md': "# Results\n\n`tables/` contains two manual-review CSVs and a summary of spending-keyword\ncounts from the notebook's saved outputs. They are included to organize\nexisting evidence, not to claim a fresh complete data run.\n\n- `destiny_negative_sample_check.csv`: the five manually labelled examples\n  in the uploaded Destiny CSV.\n- `warframe_negative_sample_check.csv`: the two contextual matches already\n  printed in the notebook, with the recorded manual labels.\n- `spending_keyword_summary.csv`: the four saved review-group counts and\n  percentages. Percentages use each group's reviews-with-text denominator.\n\nRerun with the original spreadsheets and review the results before marking\nthe figures final. Record any changed data or methods in a commit/check-in.\nPut actual figure exports in `figures/`; a chart is still pending.\n", 'results/figures/README.md': '# Figures\n\nNo final chart has been created in the supplied notebook. Save the reviewed\nvisualization here after implementing it, with a caption and a link to the\ncode and verified counts that generated it.\n', 'CONTRIBUTING.md': '# Contributing\n\n## One clear task at a time\n\n1. Choose a task from [the board](docs/task_board.md) and agree on ownership.\n2. Create a short branch such as `data/verify-counts` or `paper/methods`.\n3. Make a small change and add explanations someone else can understand.\n4. Check the relevant output and open a pull request describing the change.\n5. Ask a groupmate to review it; record decisions and real contributions.\n\nBranch names are suggestions. Work under your own account, and attribute\ncollaborations accurately. Do not rewrite history to create fake earlier\nactivity or claim another member completed work they have not confirmed.\n\n## Notebook collaboration\n\n- Coordinate before editing the same notebook at the same time.\n- Keep `notebooks/Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb` as the working analysis.\n- Preserve clear comments and meaningful outputs.\n- Make methodological changes explicitly and explain their effect on results.\n- Recheck manual label order if the data or sampled examples change.\n- Distinguish a saved-output inspection from a complete rerun on source data.\n\n## Writing and records\n\nEveryone should help with interpretation, writing and understandable code.\nAdd real completed work to [contributions](docs/contributions.md), discuss\nintermediate results in [check-ins](docs/check_ins.md), and record AI use in\n[the process log](docs/ai_usage.md). The paper should read as a shared work.\n', '.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.md': '---\nname: Project task\nabout: Organize an analysis, writing, presentation, or documentation task\ntitle: "[Task] "\nlabels: ""\nassignees: ""\n---\n\n## Task and purpose\n\nWhat will change, and which research question or assignment requirement\ndoes it help us address?\n\n## Owner and reviewer\n\nOwner: TBD\nReviewer: TBD\n\n## Done when\n\n- [ ] Concrete output completed.\n- [ ] Relevant code/output or source checked.\n- [ ] Explanation and contribution records updated.\n\n## Evidence / files\n\nLink the notebook cells, data summary, paper section, figure or commit.\nDeadline: TBD — distinguish a group target from an official due date.\n', '.github/pull_request_template.md': '## What changed and why\n\nDescribe the task and resulting behavior, finding, or document improvement.\n\n## Evidence and checks\n\nExplain what was checked. State whether source data were rerun or only\nsaved outputs were inspected. Link figures/results when relevant.\n\n## Contributions and process\n\n- Actual contributors:\n- Reviewer:\n- Task/issue link:\n- AI assistance, if used, and link to process record:\n\n## Remaining work\n\nList unresolved questions or limitations without marking them complete.\n', '.github/copilot-instructions.md': "# Repository instructions for coding assistants\n\nThis is an introductory CTS*2000 research project, not a production app.\nRead README.md, docs/project_brief.md and docs/task_board.md before editing.\n\n- Preserve the canonical working notebook and its understandable course\n  methods: loops, lists, functions, Counter, basic pandas and CSV export.\n- For organization tasks, do not refactor calculations, change samples,\n  replace manual coding with a classifier, or introduce new dependencies.\n- Never fabricate data, citations, findings, runs, deadlines, contributions,\n  GitHub handles, submissions or earlier work history.\n- The supplied results are saved-output evidence, not a claimed fresh run.\n  Do not mark the full-run task done without both source Excel files.\n- Distinguish keyword mentions, confirmed manual complaints, per-1,000-word\n  frequencies and percentages of reviews.\n- Keep the unmodified analysis in Google Colab unless a runtime change is\n  requested. google.colab.files does not work in ordinary local Jupyter.\n- The broader proposal includes gameplay/story; do not invent completed\n  analyses for them or silently decide the group's final research question.\n- Extend the AI-process record and distinguish AI assistance from student\n  contributions. Use actual model/time values only when known.\n- Preserve existing files. Reconcile scaffold collisions carefully instead\n  of overwriting unrelated work or generating competing final notebooks.\n- Use issues/PRs only in the repository selected by the user. Do not send\n  Slack messages, invite collaborators, publish a site or change visibility.\n", 'AGENTS.md': "# Repository instructions for coding assistants\n\nThis is an introductory CTS*2000 research project, not a production app.\nRead README.md, docs/project_brief.md and docs/task_board.md before editing.\n\n- Preserve the canonical working notebook and its understandable course\n  methods: loops, lists, functions, Counter, basic pandas and CSV export.\n- For organization tasks, do not refactor calculations, change samples,\n  replace manual coding with a classifier, or introduce new dependencies.\n- Never fabricate data, citations, findings, runs, deadlines, contributions,\n  GitHub handles, submissions or earlier work history.\n- The supplied results are saved-output evidence, not a claimed fresh run.\n  Do not mark the full-run task done without both source Excel files.\n- Distinguish keyword mentions, confirmed manual complaints, per-1,000-word\n  frequencies and percentages of reviews.\n- Keep the unmodified analysis in Google Colab unless a runtime change is\n  requested. google.colab.files does not work in ordinary local Jupyter.\n- The broader proposal includes gameplay/story; do not invent completed\n  analyses for them or silently decide the group's final research question.\n- Extend the AI-process record and distinguish AI assistance from student\n  contributions. Use actual model/time values only when known.\n- Preserve existing files. Reconcile scaffold collisions carefully instead\n  of overwriting unrelated work or generating competing final notebooks.\n- Use issues/PRs only in the repository selected by the user. Do not send\n  Slack messages, invite collaborators, publish a site or change visibility.\n", '.gitignore': '__pycache__/\n*.py[cod]\n.ipynb_checkpoints/\n.venv/\nvenv/\n.DS_Store\nThumbs.db\n\n# Local source data; keep the instructions tracked.\ndata/raw/*\n!data/raw/README.md\n\n# Private credentials never belong in repository commits.\n.env\n.env.*\n!.env.example\n', 'docs/ai_usage.md': "# AI assistance and process record\n\nThis file starts the process record; it is not a claim that all earlier AI\nuse is fully documented. Preserve exact prompts and outputs and add any\nmissing prior history. Do not invent model versions, timestamps or edits.\n\n## Known assistance from the project conversation\n\n| Local date | Tool | Exact model/version | Assistance | Output/evidence | Student review |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2026-11-19 | ChatGPT | Not recorded | Append Warframe negative-review output and manual comparison using existing/course methods | Included working notebook and review-check CSV | To record |\n| 2026-11-19 | ChatGPT | Not recorded | Add short comments explaining code cells | Included annotated working notebook | To record |\n| 2026-11-19 | ChatGPT | Not recorded | Create this repository organization setup | Uploaded setup_cts2000_github.py and generated scaffold | To record |\n\nExact times of the earlier interactions and inherited notebook authorship\nare not established in this setup. Add the conversation export or copies of\nthe prompts/results and identify the group's substantive changes. The\ninitial scaffold commit must not be presented as earlier development history.\n\n## Exact visible requests associated with these edits\n\n1. `ok can you add on an output for warframe withought changing any major code and using methods presented in the course github`\n2. `ok make a small comment for each important cell so groupmates know what it does`\n3. `just write the comments here`\n4. `are the numbers the cells`\n5. `ok i need a git hub to organize the work. you should have the assignment details and shi already in ur memory i need u to make an organized github i can put into github code and itll make it itself`\n\nResult locations: the supplied notebook contains the added code, comments,\ntables and saved outputs. This setup script preserves its initial bytes and\nthe generated scaffold. Add the full surrounding conversation where needed;\nsummaries do not substitute for the requested prompts and output record.\n\n## Copy for future use\n\n- Date, time and timezone:\n- Tool and model/version, if actually known:\n- Exact prompt or durable prompt-copy path:\n- Exact output / generated code or durable output path:\n- What a student checked, changed, rejected or wrote themselves:\n- Contributor and reviewer:\n- Commit / pull request:\n\nA coding assistant that runs this setup should append the exact execution\nprompt and its resulting changes to this file or a linked process record.\n", 'SETUP_NOTES.md': "# Setup notes\n\nThis repository scaffold was generated from the supplied course outline,\ngroup topic statement, latest annotated notebook and its saved results.\n\nThe setup script only creates local files. It does not create a GitHub\naccount/repository, push commits, open issues or a Projects board, invite\nteammates, submit to CourseLink, or run a new data analysis.\n\nIf an existing file differs, the script preserves it and prints its path.\nReview and merge documentation deliberately; use `docs/project_home.md`\nif an existing root README was preserved. Re-running the setup is safe and\ndoes not overwrite the group's subsequent edits.\n\nThe notebook included in the script is the version prepared for the group\nin the conversation. If a more recent notebook exists in the actual GitHub\nrepository, keep the newer work and review differences rather than replacing\nit with this embedded baseline.\n\nSources inspected: course outline pp. 4–8; topic statement pp. 1–3;\ncurrent annotated notebook; supplied Destiny sample CSV; publisher dataset\nmetadata. Source spreadsheets are not bundled and no fresh full run is\nclaimed. AI-process and contribution records remain for group completion.\n", 'results/tables/destiny_negative_sample_check.csv': 'review_text,monetization_complaint,reason\nLots of time spent with friends it was really good in the begging but now its the Free to Play game that you never stop buying especially when the sunset tons of content you Paid for! it was fun in the begging sad to see it go I hope that for any who stick around to see it end have a good time I hope the next Bungie game is not paywall simulator deluxe,True,"Repeated purchases, paywalls, and removal of paid content"\nIts a fun game to play but is so overly complicated for no reason. Not to mention all the monetization crap. Can\'t just relax and play it. Gotta get all the right gear and weapons and oh yeah better have some friends online or else 70% of the game you simply cant play. No wonder this game is dying. Bungie cant simply make a fun simple game to save their lives.,True,Explicit criticism of monetization\n"the game is dead, it has fallen off \ngarbage story, trash lore, questionable dungeons and raids\nno reason to play AND KEEP GIVING THOSE MORONS MONEY\nNO MORE DLCS STOP BUYING THEM",True,Opposes further DLC spending because of dissatisfaction\n"Microtransaction hell-hole. This game still feels great to play, then you look in the store for a PAID game and see all the stuff that should have been earned cosmetic loot from exciting missions behind (sometimes not small) monetary walls and remember that you\'re just being milked.  They give you just enough """"""""free"""""""" (not free at all you pay for every season) content to keep you engaged while clearly putting more effort into the in-game store than the game itself. It used to be that the in game store had most of the paid cosmetics available to be earned though bright dust, or paid for by silver (real money) if you couldn\'t be bothered to save up dust (fake in game money), which while annoying was fine I guess. Now almost everything is real money only. Also you cant play like 90% of the story anymore because content vaulting was deemed OK.",True,Criticises paid cosmetics and emphasis on the store\n"This has been coming for a long time.\n\nI miss you so much Destiny 😢😿 I hope someday you find safely, away from your cruel and extortionate creators.\n\n\nMe and my clan have spent thousands of hours, playing over generations of consoles. Now none of my clan remain.\n\nI don\'t mean to sound dramatic or like an old record but.\n\nI payed full price for the game on day one. And then I payed full price for DLC content, repeatedly... GREAT! 👍....\nIt was perfect! (I\'m only slightly exaggerating).\n\nAnd then it happened. 😢😭🤢🤮\n\nIt felt like we lost everything that day, the day when Beyond Light came and took everything from us. And then! AND THEN!  We were expected to pay for absolutely EVERYTHING!. Honestly, I still can\'t wrap my head around it. You seriously want me to pay for the game again?\nAnd again?\nAnd again??\nAND AGAIN???\n\nDidn\'t you get bought by Sony? Why do you need me to keep giving you handouts, just so you can remove content. And replace it, in a game EVERYONE HAD ALREADY PAYED FOR,\nIn a game that worked great, people enjoyed and nobody ask you to delete.\n\nAnd to be reskining as much as you do, holy hell!!! Stop it!\n\nI couldn\'t care less about the seasons. They make me feel sick. It\'s just the old progression system, sliced out of the game and sold back to us. Only it\'s quality is way worse. I don\'t understand how it isn\'t clearly a PAY TO WIN system.  \n\nIn your quest to not over-deliver you have significantly under-delivered in almost everything.\n\nI think It might be time to accept that the magic is never going to come back.\n\nI\'m sorry fellow Guardians, I didn\'t think it would go so wrong. 😢",True,Criticises repeated charges and removal of purchased content\n', 'results/tables/warframe_negative_sample_check.csv': 'review_text,monetization_complaint,reason\n"I wanted to play Warframe, a fast paced parkour shooter. \n\nThen I encountered quest ""The New War"" which was slowest hours long slog-fest I\'ve ever encountered, which I am locked inside.\n\nHave hated every second of it, from the slowest most awkward ninja depicted in a video game (I include old NES games) to most shittiest sneak type missions of known galaxy. Every segment seemed to have different controls and mechanics, which you never had time to actually familiarize yourself with because they had nothing to do how rest of the game plays. Not a moment has been fun or engaging game play, which is achievement in itself.\n\nWhy e.g. a glaive is suddenly working totally differently how it had worked rest of the game up until now? No idea. Why are there supposed trails to the targets you have to find which are colored like background effects in that same area and do not seem to work half of the time anyway? No idea. Why everything is suddenly a slow paced walking simulator? No idea. Why guards have vision cones but they actually see WAY past those cones unlike any enemy before them (was e.g. hiding in a container but great that they see and shoot through walls). No idea. Why when you ""die"" it takes dozens of second to even replay? No idea. Why is every weapon suddenly preselected for you? No idea. Why when you fight the ""owl"" and most of the time you spend waiting to do damage while it regenerates and you cannot do any damage to it (= boss literally designed to waste your time)? No idea.\n\nWho was that uniquely intelligent person who came up ALL that? No idea. Was it the same person who thought it was that such ""brilliant"" idea to lock new players within Duviri Paradox with no way out, if they happened to make the mistake of choosing Duviri start instead of Warframe? (Because they are new players and have no idea how the game is supposed to work, or what Duviri even is if they do not do homework before playing).\n\nIt\'s been as curios as has been base design ideas behind e.g. Total War Warhammers as example. What people have most liked about Total Wars? Sandbox style gameplay - make your own decisions how to proceed. So what should main campaigns be like? Everything BUT sandbox, constant ticking timers and forcing player to do stuff within limited time-frame. What people like in Warframe? fast paced movement and shootouts with customizable frames and weapons which suit your personal playstyle. So what should hours long main quest be like? Everything BUT previously mentioned elements. That is a very curious design philosophy behind game design, make your games less fun to play.\n\nHere\'s a free to read negative review, no need for platinum.",False,"Criticises quest design; uses spend about time waiting, not money"\n"I was banned for using a ""cheating software"" (why the hell would I cheat, with so much time and money in the game). I can assure you, I wasn\'t cheating. While my ban is lifted, this is the second time this month that something on my pc tripped their anti cheating software. So I\'m now just too afraid to touch the game, because they\'re calling me a cheater, and refuse to provide any further details, or help on what might be tripping the program so I can remove it, instead calling me a cheater and telling me to stop trying to modify the program when I go to support.\nI know that I\'m some random dude on the internet, but why the hell would I cheat on this game. I have no reason to, I\'ve done most of my farming already and just play for events / to get MR. \nAs for what I THINK is the issue, a warning to anyone: Don\'t run Stable Diffusion while playing, instead of warning you that something is ""modifying your memory"" (what the fuck does that mean) they silently ban you a week later.\n",False,"Criticises a ban and support; mentions money invested, not pricing"\n', 'results/tables/spending_keyword_summary.csv': 'game,recommendation,reviews_with_text,matching_reviews,percentage_of_reviews\nDestiny 2,Recommended,521,54,10.36\nDestiny 2,Not recommended,476,173,36.34\nWarframe,Recommended,938,62,6.61\nWarframe,Not recommended,60,2,3.33\n'}

NOTEBOOK_BASE64 = ''
NOTEBOOK_PATH = 'notebooks/Steam_Reviews_D2_VS_Warframe.ipynb'
NOTEBOOK_SHA256 = '15024839f06f3e3432de55ff753be5813d14aa1a34866d85fa634b12ee8e345b'

def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--directory", default=".", help="Repository root; defaults to current directory")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    root = Path(args.directory).resolve()
    root.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    entries = {name: text.encode("utf-8") for name, text in TEXT_FILES.items()}
    notebook_bytes = base64.b64decode(NOTEBOOK_BASE64, validate=True)
    if hashlib.sha256(notebook_bytes).hexdigest() != NOTEBOOK_SHA256:
        raise ValueError("Embedded notebook failed its integrity check")
    entries[NOTEBOOK_PATH] = notebook_bytes
    created, unchanged, preserved = [], [], []
    for name, content in entries.items():
        target = root / name
        resolved = target.resolve()
        if root not in resolved.parents or target.is_symlink():
            preserved.append(name + " (unsafe or symbolic-link path)")
            continue
        if target.exists():
            if target.is_file() and target.read_bytes() == content:
                unchanged.append(name)
            else:
                preserved.append(name)
            continue
        try:
            target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            with target.open("xb") as out:
                out.write(content)
        except FileExistsError:
            preserved.append(name)
            continue
        created.append(name)
    print(f"Created {len(created)} files; {len(unchanged)} already identical; {len(preserved)} existing paths preserved.")
    for name in created:
        print("  + " + name)
    if preserved:
        print("Review these preserved paths and merge documentation if needed:")
        for name in preserved:
            print("  = " + name)
    print("Start with README.md or docs/project_home.md.")
    print("Next: review the task board, add source spreadsheets locally, and record real contributions.")
    print("The analysis remains a Google Colab notebook; it has not been rerun by setup.")
    print("GitHub repository creation, commits/PRs, collaborator access, and course submissions are separate steps.")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
